7-**Summary for reviewers**
8-
9:- A **run plan** is the one frozen graph the scheduler runs. Each node names one pinned CC version, typed inputs and outputs, limits and the pinned check bundle and profile of that CC.
10-- Two sources produce the same run plan type through the same binder and freeze. A hand-authored **static template** (built first, the M1 default) and a **plan proposal** from the Planner (built second). See the [build plan](../3-build-plan.md).
11-- The binder rejects a bad plan before dispatch. It copies check pins from the library. Neither the template author nor the Planner can change them.
12:- After freeze there is no replan, substitution or pin change. Future values bind only to gate-accepted outputs, at dispatch.
13-- The scheduler writes one **dispatch record** per node attempt. It first turns the frozen node into one Node Execution Contract with one work subnode and one verifier subnode for each output (see [From run plan node to execution contracts](#from-run-plan-node-to-execution-contracts)).
14-- The **plan gate** accepts the bound plan. When `plan_source` is `planned`, it uses the Planner CC's pinned bundle and checking profile. When it is `static`, the template is a protected, pre-admitted record, so only deterministic checks run and no verifier runs.
15:- A failed blocking check or criterion halts the node and keeps the candidate and its evidence. Nothing retries or repairs it (decision D4, PRD 4.8.3).
16:- An infeasible or rejected plan halts the run and records a `plan_infeasible` **plan outcome**. For a planned run the host starts a new, linked Intention Compiler attempt that carries the Brief ref and the reasons. A request gets at most two plan attempts: the original and one linked new attempt (depth 1 and 2). After the second outcome the host halts and writes an escalation record ([D30](../method/decisions.md#d30)). A rejected static template halts at once with an escalation record and no new attempt. There is no repair inside the same run (decision D4, PRD 4.8.3).
