524-- Check that `cc`, `check_pins` and the input names equal the run plan node before the attempt.
525-- `attempt` is at most the node `max_attempts` (1 in M1).
526:- Nothing retries a node. A failed blocking check or criterion ends the attempt with outcome `rejected`: the node halts and keeps its candidate output and evidence. The scheduler starts no second attempt, and no worker repairs the output. A new attempt exists only when a person or a linked Intention Compiler attempt starts new work.
527-- A node whose attempt ends other than `accepted` stops only itself and the nodes that depend on it ([D49](../method/decisions.md#d49)). The scheduler marks each dependent, directly or through other nodes, `skipped` with the failed node as the reason. It writes no dispatch record for a skipped node. It keeps dispatching independent nodes in Kahn order. When no node is left to dispatch, the run ends `completed` if every node was `accepted` and `partial` otherwise. Nothing waits for a person ([D50](../method/decisions.md#d50)). A protected stop outside a node (library, plan, budget or security fault) halts the whole run.
528-- Set the dispatch record outcome from the enum above and never use another status word.
--
569-Producer: protected binder and scheduler. Consumers: host run controller, the next Intention Compiler attempt, audit. The binder writes one record when it cannot freeze a plan. It records a rejected static template or plan proposal, a Planner infeasibility reply, or a plan gate that did not advance. The run halts at once. Nothing in the same run changes the plan, swaps in the static template or retries the Planner. This follows decision D4 and answers PRD 4.8.3, which sends an infeasible plan back to the Intention Compiler.
570-
571:Next step for a planned run: the host starts a new Intention Compiler attempt that is linked to this outcome. For a rejected static template there is no automatic attempt: `linked_attempt_id` is null and the host halts with an escalation record ([D30](../method/decisions.md#d30)). The attempt receives the Brief ref (`brief_ref`) and the `reasons`, so the Compiler can change the Brief or say why it cannot. The new attempt is a new run. It may produce a changed Brief and a new plan. M1 gives a request at most two plan attempts: the original and one linked new attempt (`attempt_depth` 1 and 2). After the second outcome the host halts, writes an escalation record and sets `linked_attempt_id` to null. The limit is a fixed M1 value, and only a recorded decision changes it. The intake carries this record as `prior_plan_outcome_ref` (see [qualified-intake](field-catalog.md#qualified-intake)). The Intent and Requirement CCs read the `reasons`. Each reason must be addressed or carried forward. Intent either resolves it in the Intent IR or lists it as an uncertainty. Requirements either resolves it in the Brief or lists it in `unresolved_items`. Neither CC drops a reason silently.
